00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.990
The longest border
fence in the world today

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:17.530
is not between India and
Pakistan, or US and Mexico,

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.090
or Syria and Turkey.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:22.100
Those are all very big,
scary, long fences, but no.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.193
The longest border fence
on Earth looks like this.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:33.550
It's 5600 kilometers long.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:34.990
That's like if you were to draw a border

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.980
across the whole United
States from LA to New York

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.560
and then add another thousand miles,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.410
and this border fence doesn't
even separate two countries.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.560
Instead, this fence stretches
across some of the most

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.940
remote territory on Earth.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.990
It goes through farmlands,
deserts, across mountain ranges.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.900
But unlike most physical
barriers that we use

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.250
to divide countries, to keep people out,

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.920
this long ass border was built to keep out

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.259
one very specific thing, dingoes.

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:06.970
(dingoes howling)

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:08.216
- Here in southeast Queensland

00:02:08.216 --> 00:02:11.050
and this is the start of the dingo fence.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:14.400
Now the dingo fence was
established in the 80s.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.900
(light music)

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.290
- Okay, can we just say
something right upfront?

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:25.060
Do we all agree that
Australia is pretty wild

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.093
and a pretty unique place?

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:29.014
(woman shouting)

00:02:29.014 --> 00:02:30.218
- [Woman] Sweet Mother!

00:02:30.218 --> 00:02:31.730
(woman shouting)

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.260
- There's a reason for this.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:36.100
Back when all of this was happening,

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.100
all of these continents were
still attached to one another.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.320
But down here, Australia jumped the gun

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.390
on the whole breaking apart thing,

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.660
and it broke off from all the other land

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.550
way earlier than the others.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:53.380
- Bye bitch! (laughing)

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.550
- Because of this, all
the plants and animals

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.580
on the Australian piece of
land evolved in isolation

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.230
for a really long time, far
away from anything else.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.190
When we talk about the
seven continents, it's like,

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:09.510
North America, Asia,
Africa, and then Australia.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:13.270
It's a country, but it's
basically its own continent.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:16.960
This isolation resulted in
around 90% of the wildlife

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.070
in Australia being wholly
unique to the continent.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.470
It doesn't exist anywhere else.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.310
And it stayed this way
for a very long time,

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:27.629
until of course, these guys showed up.

00:03:27.629 --> 00:03:30.212
(hip-hop beat)

00:03:32.582 --> 00:03:36.000
Ugh, another story of these guys.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.190
It's always these guys
showing up from Europe.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:41.210
So the year is 1770 and
humans have been living

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:44.180
on Australia for 70 thousand years.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.380
But despite this, you
get this guy who shows up

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.717
and quote, "Claims possession
of the East Coast of Australia

00:03:50.717 --> 00:03:52.624
"for the British Crown."

00:03:52.624 --> 00:03:55.100
(royal music)

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.760
Soon, a bunch of British
people showing up to Australia

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.940
to set up their own colony,

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.960
and this is where the whole
pristine unique ecosystem thing

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:05.340
kinda starts to fall apart.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.640
So in addition to what
these British people did

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.100
to the Native people who were living there

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.230
for 70 thousand years.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:13.194
- [Man] Massacres, a systemic purge

00:04:13.194 --> 00:04:15.900
practiced against the
Jews in the 20th century.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.076
- The other thing the Europeans
did when they arrived here

00:04:18.076 --> 00:04:20.840
was they brought all sorts
of plants and animals

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:24.070
and a desire to do farming
and all the agriculture stuff

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:25.770
that they had been doing back in Europe.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.493
Meanwhile, Australia's 120
year old ecosystem is like,

00:04:30.537 --> 00:04:34.797
"Ugh, SMH, we had perfectly
balanced our ecology

00:04:34.797 --> 00:04:36.797
"and these guys are bringing camels?"

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.350
They also start growing a
ton of non-native plants

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.810
like sugar, and when they
started growing sugar,

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.380
there was one native species in Australia

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:46.880
that was very excited.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:48.700
That native species looked like this.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.230
It was a greyback beetle who
was stoked about the fact

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.740
that these British people
were growing sugarcane,

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:56.850
so they just started to feast
on all these sugar farms.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.010
So now the Europeans, now
independent Australians,

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:01.027
instead of being like,

00:05:01.027 --> 00:05:02.817
"Hey, maybe we shouldn't grow sugar here

00:05:02.817 --> 00:05:04.367
"because every time we grow it,

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:07.130
"it gets infested by these beetles."

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:08.137
Instead, they're like,

00:05:08.137 --> 00:05:11.800
"Let's go to Hawaii and
get a bunch of frogs."

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.320
The idea is that the frogs
would eat the beetles

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.150
and then the beetles would go away.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:17.320
Right?

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.310
But no.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:21.230
The frogs, or the cane toads specifically,

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.470
had no natural predator in Australia,

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:26.610
so they just exploded in their numbers,

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.190
and they became a pest
in and of themselves.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:32.227
- Hey look, those frogs
are eating all our crops!

00:05:32.227 --> 00:05:33.060
(Simpsons laughing)

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.640
- Okay, one more example here
that I have to tell you about

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.990
before I get back to the border fence.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.770
One more because I can't
not talk about this.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:44.770
So back in 1932, emus,
these big giant weird birds

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:48.480
were harassing the farmers, and
so the Australian government

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.160
sent out the Australian army,

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:55.060
thousands of rounds of
ammunition, to fight these emus

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:56.450
and to kill them off,

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:58.720
so that they could control the population.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.926
The best part about the story
is that the emus won the war!

00:06:01.926 --> 00:06:03.120
(birds cawing)

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:05.250
I honestly, I need to make a video

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:09.440
specifically about the emu wars,
because it's just too nuts.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.040
If you want me to do that, let me know

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.790
because I want to do that.

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:14.450
Okay, so those are all the ways

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:17.517
in which the European settlers
brought invasive species

00:06:17.517 --> 00:06:19.920
to this ecologically unique place,

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.067
and sort of started to
mess with the balance.

00:06:22.067 --> 00:06:25.090
So one of the big animals
that the British brought over

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:29.880
when they were settling
Australia were these guys, sheep.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:33.260
Sheep have wool and meat, and
were a big deal in England,

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:34.357
and so the Australians were like,

00:06:34.357 --> 00:06:36.867
"Why not bring them halfway
around the world to Australia

00:06:36.867 --> 00:06:38.700
"and start sheep farming?"

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.283
(hip hop beat)

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:46.070
Sheep farming began spreading
all around the country,

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:48.910
as Europeans settled in Australia.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:50.860
But the farmers started to realize

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:52.497
that we're not in England anymore.

00:06:52.497 --> 00:06:55.100
- No, I know we're not in Kansas.

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.584
- And their sheep were
starting to disappear.

00:06:57.584 --> 00:06:59.060
(mysterious music)

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:01.720
And it turns out that they
were being hunted and eaten

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:05.640
by an animal with a very
cool name, the dingo.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:09.340
Dingoes are kind of
like ancient wild dogs.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.892
Man, ancient wild dogs,
that should be a character

00:07:11.892 --> 00:07:14.723
in an anime film, and
it probably already is.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:20.680
Dingoes like to eat
kangaroos, and in doing so,

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:25.490
as per how ecology and
natural food systems work,

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:28.910
eating the kangaroos keeps the
kangaroo population at bay,

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:31.847
and this is very important
so that we can avoid

00:07:31.847 --> 00:07:33.790
this happening too often.

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.630
- He's getting mad that
I'm trying to pet him.

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:36.740
Okay yeah, I'm done now.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:38.180
- [Woman] I'm videotaping.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:39.886
- No I'm kidding, that's
actually not the reason

00:07:39.886 --> 00:07:41.410
why it's important.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:43.680
It's important to keep the
kangaroo population at bay

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.450
because kangaroos eat
vegetation and plants,

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:48.870
and if they grow too
large as a population,

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:50.280
they'll eat all the plants,

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:52.920
they'll mess with the food
systems for other herbivores

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:56.830
and the erosion and the soil
content, et cetera, et cetera.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:59.800
The whole ecosystem relies on this balance

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:01.247
of predator and prey.

00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:05.000
- We are all connected in
the great circle of life.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.770
- So the dingoes are important,

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:08.960
but the Europeans didn't like the dingoes

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:10.960
because they were showing up to the farms

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.750
and eating their succulent lamb chops.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:15.180
This became a big problem,

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:16.527
and eventually the farmers are like,

00:08:16.527 --> 00:08:19.177
"How am I supposed to
raise my livestock here

00:08:19.177 --> 00:08:21.067
"in this wild country with these dingoes

00:08:21.067 --> 00:08:22.750
"running around eating my sheep?"

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:26.490
Meanwhile, Australia's 120
million year old ecosystem

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:30.557
is like, "Deal with it,
Liam the sheep farmer.

00:08:30.557 --> 00:08:33.647
"We've been crafting this wild ecosystem

00:08:33.647 --> 00:08:35.227
"for a really long time,

00:08:35.227 --> 00:08:36.327
"and we're not gonna change it

00:08:36.327 --> 00:08:38.310
"just so you can have your lamb chops."

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:41.170
And Liam the sheep farmer's
like, "Hold my beer."

00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:43.788
Oh excuse me, "Hold my beer."

00:08:43.788 --> 00:08:45.690
(laughs)

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:48.700
I try not to do accents
because it's kind of cliche

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:52.370
and I can't do accents very
well, but I just love the way

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:53.970
Australians say beer.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:55.240
- Beer it is.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:09:00.030
- And thus begins another
round of Let's Outsmart Nature.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:02.130
So anyway, they start planning a fence.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:06.250
What if we cut off a giant
swath of this country

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:08.960
so that all of the dingoes
stay on that side of the fence,

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:10.020
and all the sheep farmers

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:11.970
can stay on this side of the fence?

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:13.090
So they built the fence.

00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:16.550
It took them eight years to
construct this massive fence,

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:19.740
the longest on Earth, and it worked.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:23.080
After they completed this
fence, sheep deaths plummeted,

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:25.210
and so now the farmers are very happy

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:27.570
and the Australian
government is very happy

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:29.490
because the farmers are happy.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:33.250
Meanwhile, Australia's 120
million year old ecosystem

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:34.920
is not so happy.

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:36.070
- Not happy, Jen!

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:40.000
- Okay, so remember the kangaroos?

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:41.520
- He's getting mad that
I'm trying to pet him.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:43.310
- Yeah, so on this side of the fence,

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:44.810
there's now no dingoes,

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:47.190
which means no one to eat the kangaroos.

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:49.640
So the population of kangaroos skyrockets.

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:53.170
They start eating all of
the grass and vegetation.

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:55.980
This overgrazing has left little food

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.200
for the smaller plant-eating animals

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.390
whose population is now declining,

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:03.360
and now it's also messed with
the natural nutrient cycle

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:06.560
of the soil, so now stuff
doesn't grow as well

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:07.940
in all of these soils.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.020
This change in vegetation made it easier

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:13.540
for wind to pick up sand and
create these giant dunes,

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:17.600
which then served as a wall
to redirect the wind patterns,

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:19.520
which changes the weather patterns,

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:21.200
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:24.750
You build one fence to protect sheep

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:27.020
and you trigger a ripple effect

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:30.060
that throws off a balance in nature

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:32.970
that had been literally
hundreds of millions

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:34.370
of years in the making.

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:37.030
But to the humans, it is very worth it,

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:39.050
because the Australian sheep industry

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:40.670
is worth $6.6 billion.

00:10:43.536 --> 00:10:44.430
(sighs)

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:45.750
The reason I'm making this video,

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:47.740
the reason I'm fascinated with this fence,

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:49.593
isn't just because it's so long.

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:53.640
Yes, I like borders and lines and maps,

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.820
but I also see that this fence represents

00:10:55.820 --> 00:11:00.330
an overt symbol of this
very European, Western,

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:04.020
human-centric notion of
humans controlling nature.

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:06.180
We can engineer our way out of anything.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:09.510
The Western world has helped
create this global culture

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:13.000
that says that we the humans
can do whatever we need to

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.900
to serve our needs, whether
it's food or shelter or wool.

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:19.010
And in the process, we
can ignore the needs

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:22.600
of every other organism that
shares the environment with us.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:26.060
And what we are seeing is
that there's a cost to this.

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:30.906
Over a million different
species are set to be extinct

00:11:30.906 --> 00:11:34.350
in coming decades because of our activity,

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:37.150
because we like to build
fences to protect our sheep.

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:39.080
And what's crazy is
that it doesn't totally

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:40.640
have to be this way.

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:44.670
So much of our modern culture
is sort of a blip on the map.

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:46.130
We just started.

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:47.360
This idea of going out

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:49.900
and engineering our way out of nature.

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:53.737
Remember that before the
Europeans arrived to Australia,

00:11:53.737 --> 00:11:57.496
the aboriginals had been living
there for 70 thousand years

00:11:57.496 --> 00:12:00.830
with nature, not in spite of it.

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:03.200
They didn't need to manipulate
it for their own needs

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:06.030
in a way that threw off the
balance of these ecosystems.

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:08.340
So far, we've been able
to engineer our way

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:10.850
out of quite a lot of things,

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:14.490
but this experiment of breaking
away from natural systems

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:18.020
is brand new and we have
no idea how much longer

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:19.730
the natural world will tolerate it

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:22.840
before it really bites
back against the humans

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:24.580
and puts up in our place.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:26.905
And maybe this is the wake up call we need

00:12:26.905 --> 00:12:30.900
to actually start to put into practice

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.140
what indigenous communities
have known forever,

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:36.040
which is that the only
way for long-term survival

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:40.190
as a species is if we
learn to share this Earth

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:43.408
instead of trying always to dominate it.

00:12:43.408 --> 00:12:45.908
(light music)

